The Prototype Design Pattern is a creational pattern that creates new objects
by cloning an existing object (prototype) instead of instantiating from scratch.

📌 When to Use Prototype
Use Prototype when:
Object creation is expensive (DB calls, complex setup)
You want to avoid subclass explosion
You need many similar objects with small differences
Object configuration is dynamic at runtime

🏗 Structure
Prototype (interface)
   └── clone()

ConcretePrototype
   └── implements clone()

Client
   └── uses prototype.clone()

🏢 Real Enterprise Use Cases
1️⃣ Configuration Objects
Clone base config and tweak per environment.
2️⃣ Template-based Order Creation
Duplicate an order structure and modify few fields.
3️⃣ Document Generation Systems
Clone document templates.
4️⃣ Caching Expensive Objects
Pre-configured objects reused via cloning.


🎯 When NOT to Use It
If object structure is simple
If deep cloning is complex
If object contains circular references
If immutability is preferred